Now we got another failure of the job in prelive because of the new checks: [https://bolt-geo.cloud.databricks.com/compute/sparkui/0901-000149-4xhz53il/driver-logs?o=1492481942411573|https://bolt-geo.cloud.databricks.com/compute/sparkui/0901-000149-4xhz53il/driver-logs?o=1492481942411573]

We investigate the issue more, and even if in the source code of the accumulator(written in Scala) things looked like they were thread-safe(synchronized) we challenged that and wrote a little bit of code to test it.

{noformat}CollectionAccumulator<String> pathsAccumulator = sparkSession.getSparkContext().sc().collectionAccumulator("TMP");
    List<String> inputs = new ArrayList<>();
    for(int i=0; i< 1000; i++){
      inputs.add("input-" + i);
    }
    inputs.parallelStream().forEach(input -> {
       pathsAccumulator.add(input);
    });
    logger.info("TMP accumulator size: {}", pathsAccumulator.value().size());{noformat}

The shocking result: 720. So this is the culprit.

We improved the code a little bit like this

{noformat}CollectionAccumulator<String> pathsAccumulator = sparkSession.getSparkContext().sc().collectionAccumulator("TMP");
    List<String> inputs = new ArrayList<>();
    for(int i=0; i< 1000; i++){
      inputs.add("input-" + i);
    }
    inputs.parallelStream().forEach(input -> {
     synchronized (pathsAccumulator) {
       pathsAccumulator.add(input);
     }
    });
    logger.info("TMP accumulator size: {}", pathsAccumulator.value().size());{noformat}

and the new results was 1000. 

The final solution is to synchronize the {{add}} operation on the accumulator to make it thread-safe.

