Hi [~accountid:5f4ce505d0884f0049200148] [~accountid:628dd71cf2ee4a0069dfba72] , thanks for laying out the flow, and apologies for missing your response. From what you’ve explained, it seems that we do need a Data Sharing Agreement (DSA) alongside the NDA.

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*Why this is personal data*
{panel}

* The OMV loyalty card number on its own looks like a technical ID. But once we link it to our driver records via the Google Form, it becomes a direct key to an identifiable driver.
* The data OMV will send back (fuel consumption, type, location) is therefore driver-level personal data once it enters Bolt’s systems. Under GDPR, identifiability is assessed in the context of the controller, so the fact that _we_ can connect it to a person is what matters.

{panel:bgColor=#ffebe6}
*Why NDA alone is not enough*
{panel}

* The NDA only covers confidentiality. It does not address how OMV must handle personal data, what happens in case of a breach, retention limits, or drivers’ GDPR rights.
* Without a DSA, we would have no enforceable framework to ensure OMV processes the data properly. If something goes wrong, Bolt would carry the compliance / reputational risk.



The DSA is a short standard document we use in all similar partnerships. It does not add operational burden but protects Bolt in case OMV mishandles driver data.

If you do not want a DSA, I can add a light-touch clause to the NDA to cover data sharing. Let me know your thoughts.

